markdown <<MARKDOWN
<h1>Section 3: Making Predictions</h1>

<div style="display: inline-block; background-color: #E4E2F8; color: #1F1976; font-weight: bold; font-size: 13px; padding: 4px 12px; border-radius: 10px; margin-bottom: 12px;">Aligns to curriculum Lessons 3-4</div>

<p>This section prepares you to teach two curriculum lessons. Students first learn to read and interpret data, categorical and numerical, then use that foundation to make the central conceptual leap of the whole unit: turning data into a prediction, and recognizing whether that prediction is a category or a number.</p>

<div style="background-color: #F7F7FB; border-left: 4px solid #4C42CF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; margin-bottom: 10px;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5;"><b>Curriculum Lesson 3:</b> Describing Data</div>
  <a href="PASTE_UNIT_2_LESSON_3_URL_HERE" style="display: inline-block; background-color: #4C42CF; color: #FFFFFF; font-weight: bold; padding: 6px 14px; border-radius: 8px; text-decoration: none; margin-top: 6px; font-size: 13px;">View Curriculum Lesson 3</a>
</div>

<div style="background-color: #F7F7FB; border-left: 4px solid #4C42CF; border-radius: 8px; padding: 12px 16px; margin-bottom: 16px;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5;"><b>Curriculum Lesson 4:</b> Prediction Models</div>
  <a href="PASTE_UNIT_2_LESSON_4_URL_HERE" style="display: inline-block; background-color: #4C42CF; color: #FFFFFF; font-weight: bold; padding: 6px 14px; border-radius: 8px; text-decoration: none; margin-top: 6px; font-size: 13px;">View Curriculum Lesson 4</a>
</div>

<hr>

<p><b>In this section, you'll:</b></p>
<div style="margin-bottom: 6px; font-size: 16px;">&bull; Tell categorical and numerical data apart, and recognize correlation and outliers in a real scatter plot</div>
<div style="margin-bottom: 6px; font-size: 16px;">&bull; Meet Anita's Parlor, the running example for this whole section, and see why a small business owner needs predictions in the first place</div>
<div style="margin-bottom: 6px; font-size: 16px;">&bull; Try the same regression and classification widgets students use themselves</div>
<div style="font-size: 16px;">&bull; Get ahead of two real misconceptions: mistaking a business decision for a prediction question, and telling regression and classification apart</div>

<div style="background-color: #E4E2F8; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 16px 0;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #222222; line-height: 1.5;"><b>&#128161; Notice as you explore:</b> this section's two lessons share one real test worth repeating to students all year: is the output a category, or a number? That single question is what regression and classification actually come down to.</div>
</div>

<hr>

<div style="display: flex; gap: 6px; margin-top: 24px; max-width: 700px;">
